From 6bf87e630d4877704936921d819436871cb52de0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Gorbachenko <alex.gorbachenko@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 31 May 2026 08:34:20 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Pass Vec<u8> by ownership through write_to chain
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Changes the PacketConn::write_to signature from &[u8] to Vec<u8>,
eliminating a redundant buf.to_vec() allocation+memcpy per outbound
packet.

Background:
  The encrypted layer holds data in an owned Vec<u8> (from session
  encryption).  Passing it by reference through write_to forced an
  extra clone inside PacketConnImpl (buf.to_vec()).  With 3500 pps
  at 40 Mbps, this is ~3500 × 1.5 KB = 5 MB/s of unnecessary
  allocations and copies.

Fix:
  Changed the trait signature and all 3 implementations
  (PacketConnImpl, EncryptedPacketConn, SignedPacketConn) from
  write_to(&self, buf: &[u8], addr) to write_to(&self, buf: Vec<u8>,
  addr).  The owned Vec moves directly into TrafficPacket::new
  without copying.

  All callers already hold owned Vec<u8> data — the change is
  mechanical.

Breaking change:
  Pub trait PacketConn.  Only 3 implementations exist, all within
  the ironwood crate.  No external implementors.

At 40 Mbps on hAP ax³ (+jemalloc +cs-v1 +cortex-a53 +cpu3):
  Before: ~27% CPU
  After:   24% CPU  (-3 p.p.)

The optimisation is architecture-independent — saves one heap
allocation and one memcpy per outbound packet on any platform.
